"Flipping Edge-Labelled Triangulations (https://arxiv.org/html/1310.1166)\nciteturn9507view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1310.1166\",\"lineno\":46}); Total lines: 467\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Background L18:   4. cite8†3 Convex polygons L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Lower bound L20:     2. cite10†3.2 Simultaneous flips L21:   5. cite11†4 Connections between flips and sorting models L22:   6. cite12†5 Spiral polygons L23:   7. cite13†6 Lower bound on flips in edge-labelled polygons L24:   8. cite14†7 Combinatorial triangulations L25:     1. cite15†7.1 Lower bound L26:     2. cite16†7.2 Simultaneous flips L27:   9. cite17†8 Conclusions L28:   10. cite18†References L29: cite19†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L30: \nL31: arXiv:1310.1166v2 [cs.CG] 04 Mar 2016\nL32: # Flipping Edge-Labelled Triangulations\nL33: \nL34: Journal: Computational Geometry: Theory and Applications We dedicate this paper to Ferran Hurtado, whose creativity in research and seminal work on flips continues to inspire us all.\nL35: Prosenjit Bose Email: jit@scs.carleton.ca Address: School of Computer Science, Carleton University, Ottawa ON, Canada    Anna Lubiw Email: alubiw@uwaterloo.ca Address: School of Computer Science, University of Waterloo, Waterloo ON, Canada    Vinayak Pathak Email: vpathak@uwaterloo.ca Address: School of Computer Science, University of Waterloo, Waterloo ON, Canada    Sander Verdonschot Email: sander@cg.scs.carleton.ca Address: School of Computer Science, Carleton University, Ottawa ON, Canada\nL36: ###### Abstract\nL37: \nL38: Flips in triangulations have received a lot of attention over the past decades. However, the problem of tracking where particular edges go during the flipping process has not been addressed. We examine this question by attaching unique labels to the triangulation edges. We introduce the concept of the orbit of an edge $e$, which is the set of all edges reachable from $e$ via flips.\nL39: We establish the first upper and lower bounds on the diameter of the flip graph in this setting. Specifically, we prove tight $\\Theta(n\\log n)$ bounds for edge-labelled triangulations of $n$-vertex convex polygons and combinatorial triangulations, contrasting with the $\\Theta(n)$ bounds in their respective unlabelled settings. The $\\Omega(n\\log n)$ lower bound for the convex polygon setting might be of independent interest, as it generalizes lower bounds on certain sorting models.\nL40: When simultaneous flips are allowed, the upper bound for convex polygons decreases to $O(\\log^{2}n)$, although we no longer have a matching lower bound.\nL41: Moving beyond convex polygons, we show that edge-labelled triangulated polygons with a single reflex vertex can have a disconnected flip graph. This is in sharp contrast with the unlabelled case, where the flip graph is connected for any triangulated polygon. For spiral polygons, we provide a complete characterization of the orbits. This allows us to decide connectivity of the flip graph of a spiral polygon in linear time.\nL42: We also prove an upper bound of $O(n^{2})$ on the diameter of each connected component, which is optimal in the worst case. We conclude with an example of a non-spiral polygon whose flip graph has diameter $\\Omega(n^{3})$.\nL43: ###### Keywords:\nL44: \nL45: Flip, Convex Polygon, Spiral Polygon, Quadrilateral Graph, Simultaneous Flip\nL46: ## 1 Introduction\nL47: The problem of reconfiguring one triangulation to another is well-studied for combinatorial triangulations, triangulations of points sets, and triangulations of polygons. In all these settings, the basic reconfiguration operation is the flip operation that removes one edge of the triangulation and adds another edge to obtain a new triangulation. Not every edge can always be flipped. The constraints on the edges involved in a flip depend on the setting.\nL48: The geometric setting refers to a triangulation of a point set (a maximal set of non-crossing edges joining pairs of points) or polygon (a maximal set of non-crossing chords inside the polygon). In both cases, a flip removes one diagonal of a convex quadrilateral and replaces it by the other diagonal. In the combinatorial setting, a triangulation is a maximal planar graph with the clockwise order of edges around each vertex specified.\nL49: Here, a flip removes one edge and replaces it by the other diagonal of the resulting quadrilateral, as long as the new edge is not already an edge of the triangulation. When we replace an edge $e$ by an edge $f$ with a flip, we say that $e$ flips to $f$.\nL50: Another way to view reconfigurations via flips is through the flip graph, which has a vertex for each triangulation and an edge between two vertices if their corresponding triangulations differ by a single flip. The most important property of flips is that they can be used to reconfigure any triangulation into any other triangulation of the same point set, polygon, or vertex set—that is, the flip graph is connected.\nL51: Wagner [cite20†27 ] initiated the study of flips in 1936, by proving that the flip graph is connected in the combinatorial setting. For points sets, connectivity of the flip graph is a consequence of Lawson’s result [cite21†20 ] that any triangulation of a point set can be flipped to the Delaunay triangulation, which then acts as a “canonical” triangulation from which every triangulation can be reached.\nL52: For triangulations of a polygon, the constrained Delaunay triangulation can be used in the same way to prove that the flip graph is connected [cite22†3 ]. In Section cite7†2 , we discuss these results in more detail, along with bounds on the number of flips required, i.e., bounds on the diameter of the flip graph.\nL53: Reconfiguration of triangulations via flips is of theoretical interest in the study of associahedra [cite23†25 ] and mixing [cite24†21 ]. It also has more practical benefits in mesh generation and for finding triangulations that optimize certain quality measures [cite22†3 , cite25†10 ]. For a broader overview of the literature on flips, we refer the reader to a survey by Bose and Hurtado [cite26†5 ].\nL54: Despite the extensive literature on flips in many different settings, to our knowledge, no one has specifically studied where individual edges move during the course of a sequence of flips in any setting. We say that an edge $f$ is reachable from an edge $e$ if there is a sequence of edges $e,\\ldots,f$, such that for every two consecutive edges $a$ and $b$ in the sequence, there exists a triangulation in which $a$ flips to $b$.\nL55: We define the orbit of an edge $e$ to be the set of all edges reachable from $e$. This gives rise to a very natural question: What is the orbit of an edge? Although every triangulation can be flipped to every other, the orbits of edges can be very different. For example, some polygons have a unique triangulation, in which case the orbits are all singletons. At the other extreme, a convex polygon has a single orbit containing all the diagonals.\nL56: In the geometric setting, the orbits are exactly the connected components of a graph introduced by Eppstein [cite27†11 ] as the quadrilateral graph. This graph has a vertex for every diagonal and an edge between two vertices if their corresponding diagonals $e$ and $f$ intersect and their endpoints form a convex quadrilateral that is empty.\nL57: An intriguing question is how orbits combine. In other words, we want to track where multiple edges go during a sequence of flips. To address this question, we study flips in edge-labelled triangulations: triangulations of a point set, polygon, or vertex set where each edge has a unique label from $\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$, where $m$ is the number of edges. If we flip an edge of an edge-labelled triangulation, the new edge is assigned the label of the removed edge.\nL58: In particular, this means that the set of edge labels is preserved throughout any flip sequence. In the geometric setting, we often omit the labels on the edges of the convex hull or on the boundary of the polygon, as these can never be flipped.\nL59: Given two edge-labelled triangulations $T$ and $T^{\\prime}$ of the same point set, polygon, or vertex set, it is not always possible to transform $T$ to $T^{\\prime}$ via a sequence of flips. For example, when a polygon has a unique triangulation, no flips are possible, so if the labellings of $T$ and $T^{\\prime}$ differ, no flip sequence can transform one to the other.\nL60: A necessary condition for such a flip sequence to exist is that each edge with label $i$ in $T^{\\prime}$ must be in the orbit of the edge with label $i$ in $T$. We conjecture that this condition is also sufficient.\nL61: ###### Orbit Conjecture.\nL62: \nL63: Given two edge-labelled triangulations, there is a sequence of flips that transforms one into the other if and only if for every label, the initial and final edge with that label lie in the same orbit.\nL64: The Orbit Conjecture is known to hold in some cases. A result by Eppstein [cite27†11 ] implies that the Orbit Conjecture holds for triangulations of point sets with no empty pentagon. These point sets are highly degenerate since every set of ten points in general position contains an empty pentagon. Eppstein showed that in this case every orbit is a tree, and each triangulation contains exactly one edge from each orbit. There are a few other results of a similar flavour. Cano et al.\nL65: [cite28†8 ] proved the analogous statement for edge-labelled non-maximal plane graphs, although instead of flips, they “rotate\" edges around one of their endpoints. Hernando et al. [cite29†16 ] proved the analogous statement for edge-labelled spanning trees of an underlying graph, where the “flip\" operation consists of removing an edge and replacing it elsewhere with the same label while maintaining connectivity. They showed that the orbits are exactly the 2-connected components of the underlying graph.\nL66: Pathak [cite30†22 ] generalized this result to labelled bases of a matroid.\nL67: Edge-labelled flips in triangulations of a convex polygon were independently studied by Araujo-Pardo et al. [cite31†1 ], who mainly focused on the combinatorial properties of the flip graph. In the unlabelled setting, the flip graph is known to be isomorphic to the 1-skeleton of a polyhedron, called the associahedron. Araujo-Pardo et al. proved that this is also the case for the edge-labelled setting, and called the resulting polyhedron the colourful associahedron.\nL68: As part of their proof, they show that the flip graph of edge-labelled triangulations of a convex polygon is connected, which implies the Orbit Conjecture for this setting. A careful analysis of their argument shows that it gives an upper bound on the diameter of the flip graph that is quadratic in the number of vertices of the polygon. In addition, they show that the colourful associahedron covers the regular associahedron and fully characterize its automorphism group.\nL69: In this paper we address the Orbit Conjecture for edge-labelled triangulations of polygons and edge-labelled combinatorial triangulations. We give an alternative proof for the connectivity of the flip graph of edge-labelled triangulations of an $n$-vertex convex polygon that shows that the diameter is $O(n\\log n)$, and we provide a matching $\\Omega(n\\log n)$ lower bound, which contrasts with the $\\Theta(n)$ bound for unlabelled flips [cite23†25 ].\nL70: We also consider what happens when we allow multiple edges to be flipped simultaneously. We prove that the simultaneous flip distance between two edge-labelled triangulations of a convex polygon is $O(\\log^{2}n)$, in contrast with the $\\Theta(\\log n)$ bound established for the unlabelled setting [cite32†13 ].\nL71: After convex polygons, we consider spiral polygons (polygons with at most one reflex chain). These may have multiple orbits, so the flip graph is not necessarily connected. We again prove the Orbit Conjecture, and give a tight $\\Theta(n^{2})$ worst case bound on the diameter of each connected component of the flip graph. We also characterize the orbits, and show how to test in linear time whether there is a flip sequence that transforms one given edge-labelled triangulation into another.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPop-Stack Operators for Torsion Classes and Cambrian Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03959v1)\nciteturn9507view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03959v1\",\"lineno\":979}); Total lines: 1931\nL931: \\par{{}}\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\\hbox{\\hbox{{\\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {{}{}{{\nL932: {}{}}}{\nL933: {}{}}\nL934: {{}{{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{{}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{}{{}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}\nL935: {\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nL938: {{}}\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\\hbox{\\hbox{{\\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {{}{}{{\nL939: {}{}}}{\nL940: {}{}}\nL941: {{}{{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{{}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{}{{}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}\nL942: {\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nL943: }{{{{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}\\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \\lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{-25.92638pt}{-1.40277pt}\\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -35.87 -1.94)} \\pgfsys@hbox{58}\\lxSVG@closescope }}}\nL944: \\lxSVG@closescope }}}\nL945: {{}}\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\\hbox{\\hbox{{\\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {{}{}{{\nL946: {}{}}}{\nL947: {}{}}\nL948: {{}{{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{{}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{}{{}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}\nL949: {\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nL950: }{{{{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}\\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \\lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{-4.39006pt}{3.533pt}\\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.07 4.89)} \\pgfsys@hbox{58}\\lxSVG@closescope }}}\nL951: \\lxSVG@closescope }}}\nL952: \\par{\nL953: {}{}{}}{}\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\\hbox{\\hbox{{\\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {\nL954: {}{}{}}{}{\nL955: {}{}{}}\nL956: {{{{{}}{\nL957: {}{}}{}{}{{}{}}}}}{}{{{{{}}{\nL958: {}{}}{}{}{{}{}}}}}{{}}{}{}{}\\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \\lxSVG@setlinewidth{\\the\\pgflinewidth}\\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.8pt} \\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces{}\\lxSVG@stroke\\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 18.29 0 L -18.29 0}{fill:none} \\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nL959: \\lxSVG@closescope \\lxSVG@closescope }}}\nL960: \\lxSVG@closescope {\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\\hss}\\lxSVG@discardpath\\lxSVG@closescope \\hss}}\\lxSVG@closescope\\endpgfpicture}}\\end{array}$​​​ (if $m=2$, then there is no edge). Let $c=s_{1}s_{2}$. The $c$-sortable elements of $I_{2}(m)$ are $s_{2}$ and the $m+1$ elements $e,s_{1},s_{1}s_{2},s_{1}s_{2}s_{1},\\ldots,w_{\\circ}$.\nL961: For each $w\\in W$, the set $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}\\cap\\{v\\in W\\mid v\\leq w\\}$ has a unique maximal element in the weak order; we denote this element by $\\pi_{\\downarrow}^{c}(w)$. The map $\\pi_{\\downarrow}^{c}$ is a surjective lattice homomorphism from $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$ to $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$, so $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$ is a lattice quotient of $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$ [cite89†Rea06 , cite90†Rea07 ].\nL962: The fibers of $\\pi_{\\downarrow}^{c}$ are the equivalence classes of an equivalence relation on $W$ known as the $c$-Cambrian congruence.\nL963: According to [cite58†Def22a , Theorem 3.2], we have\nL964: \nL965: (6.3)  |  | $$\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}=\\pi_{\\downarrow}^{c}\\circ\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W)}.$$  |\nL966: \nL967: The following lemma, which is immediate from the definition of a $c$-sortable element (see also [cite90†Rea07 , Lemmas 2.4 & 2.5]), gives a recursive characterization of $c$-sortable elements.\nL968: ###### Lemma 6.3.\nL969: \nL970: Let $W$ be a finite Coxeter group, and let $\\mathsf{c}$ be a reduced word for a Coxeter element $c$ of $W$. Let $s$ be the first letter in $s$. Let $w\\in W$. If $\\ell(sw)>\\ell(w)$, then $w$ is $c$-sortable if and only if it is an $(sc)$-sortable element of the standard parabolic subgroup $W_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}}$. If $\\ell(sw)<\\ell(w)$, then $w$ is $c$-sortable if and only if $sw$ is $(scs)$-sortable.\nL971: We will also make use of the following characterization of $c$-sortable elements in terms of canonical join representations.\nL972: ###### Lemma 6.4.\nL973: \nL974: Let $c$ be a Coxeter element of a finite Coxeter group $W$. An element $w\\in W$ is $c$-sortable if and only if every element of the canonical join representation of $w$ in $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$ is $c$-sortable. If $w$ is $c$-sortable, then the canonical join representation of $w$ in $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$ is equal to the canonical join representation of $w$ in $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$.\nL975: ###### Proof.\nL976: \nL977: According to [cite162†RS11 , Proposition 8.2], if $w$ is $c$-sortable, then each element of its canonical join representation is $c$-sortable. On the other hand, if each element of the canonical join representation of $w$ is $c$-sortable, then $w$ is $c$-sortable because $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$ is a sublattice of the weak order. ∎\nL978: ### 6.3. Cambrian lattices as lattices of torsion classes\nL979: Suppose for this subsection that $W$ is crystallographic (i.e., $W$ is not of type $H_{3}$, $H_{4}$, or $I_{2}(m)$ for $m\\notin\\{3,4,6\\}$). Fix a Coxeter element $c\\in W$.\nL980: Associated to the acyclic orientation $Q_{c}$ of $c$ is a finite-dimensional algebra $KQ_{c}$ known as the tensor algebra of $Q_{c}$ (More precisely, the definition of $KQ_{c}$ depends on a choice of symmetrizable Cartan matrix with Weyl group $W$.) In this subsection, we recall the properties of $KQ_{c}$ that we will use to interpret cite91†Theorem 5.10 in terms of Coxeter combinatorics.\nL981: Notably, the definition of the algebra $KQ_{c}$ is not needed in order to state these results and is thus omitted from this paper. Readers interested in more details are referred to [cite164†DR76 ].\nL982: Recall the notation of the projective modules $P(i)$ and simple modules $S(i)$ from cite12†Section 3 . The indexing of these modules can be chosen so that $\\underline{\\mathrm{dim}}(S(i))=e_{i}=\\beta_{s_{i}}$ for all $i\\in[n]$. We fix this indexing for the remainder of this section. For $i\\in[n]$, we then denote\nL983: \nL984: (6.4)  |  | $$\\rho_{i}=s_{n}s_{n-1}\\cdots s_{i+1}(e_{i}).$$  |\nL985: Note that $\\{\\rho_{i}\\mid i\\in[n]\\}=\\mathrm{inv}(c^{-1})$. The roots $\\rho_{i}$ are sometimes called projective roots, a name that is justified by cite165†Item 5 in cite166†Proposition 6.5 below.\nL986: \nL987: We now recall the following well-known properties of the algebra $KQ_{c}$. Note that cite167†Items 2 , cite168†3 and cite169†4 together constitute Gabriel’s Theorem. These results can be found in the classical references [cite170†DR75 , cite164†DR76 ].\nL988: ###### Proposition 6.5.\nL989: \nL990:   1. (1)\nL991: \nL992: The algebra $KQ_{c}$ is hereditary.\nL993: \nL994:   2. (2)\nL995: \nL996: The algebra $KQ_{c}$ is representation-finite.\nL997: \nL998:   3. (3)\nL999: \nL1000: The association $M\\mapsto\\underline{\\mathrm{dim}}M$ is a bijection from the set of (isomorphism classes of) indecomposable modules in $\\mods KQ_{c}$ to the set $\\Phi_{W}^{+}$ of positive roots.\nL1001: \nL1002:   4. (4)\nL1003: \nL1004: Every indecomposable module in $\\mods KQ_{c}$ is a brick.\nL1005: \nL1006:   5. (5)\nL1007: For $i\\in[n]$, the dimension vector of the indecomposable projective module ${P(i)\\in\\mods KQ_{c}}$ is $\\underline{\\mathrm{dim}}P(i)=\\rho_{i}$.\nL1008: ###### Remark 6.6.\nL1009: \nL1010: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors KQ_{c}$ be a torsion class that satisfies $\\mathrm{Ext}_{KQ_{c}}^{1}(X,X^{\\prime})=0$ for all ${X,X^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})}$. Then cite169†Item 4 in cite166†Proposition 6.5 implies that there exists a nonzero projective module $P\\in\\mathcal{T}$ if and only if there exists an indecomposable projective module $P(i)\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL1011: We now recall the explicit bijection between the Cambrian lattice $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$ and the lattice of torsion classes $\\tors(KQ_{c})$ established in [cite66†IT09 ]. Note that while the result is only proved explicitly for the simply-laced case in [cite66†IT09 ], it is remarked in [cite66†IT09 , Section 4.4] that the results can be generalized using folding arguments.\nL1012: ###### Theorem 6.7 ([cite66†IT09 , Theorem 4.3]).\nL1013: \nL1014: There is a lattice isomorphism $\\varphi_{c}\\colon\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}\\rightarrow\\tors KQ_{c}$ characterized by the condition that\nL1015: \nL1016:  | $$\\varphi_{c}(w)\\cap\\brick KQ_{c}=\\{M\\in\\brick KQ_{c}\\mid\\underline{\\mathrm{dim}}M\\in\\mathrm{inv}(w)\\}$$  |\nL1017: \nL1018: for every $c$-sortable element $w\\in W$.\nL1019: ## 7. The Image of Pop-Stack on a Cambrian Lattice\nL1020: In this section, we recast cite91†Theorem 5.10 as a combinatorial description of the image of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}$ for any finite irreducible Coxeter group $W$ and any Coxeter element $c$. As we discuss in the proof of cite92†Theorem 7.8 , the results of cite19†Section 5 only yield a proof for $W$ of crystallographic type, while the non-crystallographic types can be verified by computer.\nL1021: Thus, when not directly specified, we assume that $W$ is an arbitrary finite Coxeter group (not necessarily crystallographic). When $W$ is crystallographic, we use the notation $KQ_{c}$ as in cite26†Section 6.3 . In any case, we denote by $c$ a fixed Coxeter element of $W$.\nL1022: We first give an interpretation of the Ext-orthogonality condition in cite91†Theorem 5.10 cite155†1 . For each $w\\in\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$, cite171†Lemmas 5.12 and cite172†6.7 say that the torsion class $\\varphi_{c}(w)$ satisfies the Ext-orthogonality condition if and only if $[\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}(w),w]$ is Boolean. cite93†Theorem 7.6 below provides several equivalent conditions characterizing when this holds. Before we state and prove this result, we require some lemmas.\nL1023: ###### Lemma 7.1.\nL1024: \nL1025: Let $L$ be a finite lattice, and let $x\\in L$. If the interval $[\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{L}(x),x]$ is distributive, then it is Boolean.\nL1026: ###### Proof.\nL1027: \nL1028: Suppose $[\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{L}(x),x]$ is distributive, and let $P$ be a finite poset such that $[\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{L}(x),x]$ is isomorphic to $J(P)$. The bottom and top elements of $J(P)$ are $\\emptyset$ and $P$, respectively, so ${\\emptyset=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{J(P)}(P)=\\bigcap_{y\\in\\max(P)}(P\\setminus\\{y\\})}$, where $\\max(P)$ is the set of maximal elements of $P$. This implies that $P=\\max(P)$, so $P$ is an antichain. Hence, $J(P)$ is Boolean. ∎\nL1029: ###### Lemma 7.2.\nL1030: \nL1031: If $L$ is a finite semidistributive lattice with $k$ coatoms, then $|L|\\geq 2^{k}$.\nL1032: \nL1033: ###### Proof.\nL1034: \nL1035: The map $v\\mapsto\\mathcal{D}(v)$ is a bijection from $L$ to the canonical join complex of $L$. Since $|\\mathcal{D}(\\hat{1})|=k$, the canonical join complex of $L$ must have at least $2^{k}$ faces. ∎\nL1036: \nL1037: The next result follows from [cite173†Rea04 , Lemma 3.8]; we include a short self-contained proof.\nL1038: ###### Lemma 7.3 ([cite173†Rea04 , Lemma 3.8]).\nL1039: \nL1040: If $y_{1}\\lessdot z_{1}$ and $y_{2}\\lessdot z_{2}$ are two edges of $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$ with the same shard label, then $z_{1}y_{1}^{-1}=z_{2}y_{2}^{-1}$.\nL1041: ###### Proof.\nL1042: \nL1043: Let $t_{1}=z_{1}y_{1}^{-1}$ and $t_{2}=z_{2}y_{2}^{-1}$. cite174†Proposition 6.1 tells us that the join-irreducible element $j_{y_{1},z_{1}}$ has $t_{1}$ as a left inversion while $(j_{y_{1},z_{1}})_{*}$ does not have $t_{1}$ as a left inversion. If follows that $t_{1}$ is the unique cover reflection of $j_{y_{1},z_{1}}$. Similarly, $t_{2}$ is the unique cover reflation of $j_{y_{2},z_{2}}$. Since $j_{y_{1},z_{1}}=j_{y_{2},z_{2}}$, we have $t_{1}=t_{2}$. ∎\nL1044: The following lemma is a special case of [cite52†Rea11 , Proposition 5.7].\nL1045: ###### Lemma 7.4 ([cite52†Rea11 , Proposition 5.7]).\nL1046: \nL1047: If $w$ and $z$ are elements of $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$ such that the canonical join representation of $w$ contains that of $z$, then the set of shard labels of edges in the interval $[\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W)}(z),z]$ is contained in the set of shard labels of the edges in the interval $[\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W)}(w),w]$.\nL1048: ###### Lemma 7.5.\nL1049: \nL1050: Suppose $w$ is a $c$-sortable element of $W$ whose descents commute. The interval $[\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W)}(w),w]$ of $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$ is Boolean, and $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}(w)$ is $c$-sortable.\nL1051: ###### Proof.\nL1052: The fact that the interval $[\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W)}(w),w]$ is Boolean is immediate from the observation that it consists of the elements of the form $wv^{-1}$ such that $v$ is the product of some subset of $\\mathrm{Des}(w)$. Let us now show that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W)}(w)$ is $c$-sortable. Our proof will proceed by induction on $|S|$ and $\\ell(w)$ (the base cases are trivial).\nL1053: To ease notation, let us write $w^{\\prime}=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W)}(w)$.\nL1054: Let $s$ be the first letter in some reduced word for $c$. Suppose first that $\\ell(sw)>\\ell(w)$. According to cite175†Lemma 6.3 , $w$ is an $(sc)$-sortable element of $W_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}}$. By induction, $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}})}(w)$ is an $(sc)$-sortable element of $W_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}}$. Invoking cite175†Lemma 6.3 again, we find that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}})}(w)$ is $c$-sortable.\nL1055: The desired result now follows from the fact that $w^{\\prime}=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}})}(w)$.\nL1056: Now assume that $\\ell(sw)<\\ell(w)$. Then $(sw)^{-1}\\leq w^{-1}$, so every left inversion of $(sw)^{-1}$ is a left inversion of $w^{-1}$. Hence, every right inversion of $sw$ is a right inversion of $w$. It follows that $\\mathrm{Des}(sw)\\subseteq\\mathrm{Des}(w)$. We consider two cases.\n"

